f="/encyclopedia/hierotheos"><span class="enc-name">Hierotheos</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hierotheos was a Byzantine monk consecrated bishop of 'Turkia', the land of the Hungarians, in the mid-10th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/high-commission-territories"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/high-commission-territories.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">High Commission Territories</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The High Commission Territories were the three British dependencies of Basutoland, the Bechuanaland Protectorate and Swaziland, governed through the British High…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/high-court-of-australia"><span class="enc-name">High Court of Australia</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The High Court of Australia is the highest court of the Commonwealth of Australia, provided for in the federal constitution of 1901 and established in 1903.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/high-king-of-ireland"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/high-king-of-ireland.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">High King of Ireland</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The High King of Ireland, Ard Rí in Irish, was the title claimed by kings who asserted supremacy over the island's many kingdoms.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/high-priest-of-amun"><span class="enc-name">High Priest of Amun</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The High Priest of Amun was the chief priest of the god Amun at Thebes, controlling the god's vast temple estates.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/highland-clearances"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/highland-clearances.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Highland Clearances</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Highland Clearances were the eviction of large numbers of tenants from the Scottish Highlands and islands, mainly between about 1750 and 1860.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/highland-east-cushitic-languages"><span class="enc-name">Highland East Cushitic languages</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Highland East Cushitic languages are a branch of the Cushitic family spoken in the highlands of southern Ethiopia, in and around the Rift Valley.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/higo-province"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/higo-province.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Higo Province</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Higo Province was an old province of Japan in central Kyushu, corresponding to modern Kumamoto Prefecture.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hiiumaa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hiiumaa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hiiumaa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Hiiumaa is the second-largest island of Estonia, lying in the Baltic Sea north of Saaremaa, and known in German and Swedish as Dagö.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hijikata-toshizo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hijikata-toshizo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hijikata Toshizō</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hijikata Toshizō was a Japanese swordsman and vice-commander of the Shinsengumi, the special police force that defended the shogunate in Kyoto in the 1860s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hijra"><span class="enc-name">Hijra</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hijra was the migration of the Prophet Muhammad and his followers from Mecca to Medina in 622 CE, which marks the start of the Islamic calendar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hijra-of-usman-dan-fodio"><span class="enc-name">Hijra of Usman dan Fodio</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hijra of Usman dan Fodio was the migration of the reformer and his followers to Gudu in 1804, modelled on the Prophet Muhammad's hijra.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-aceh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hikayat-aceh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hikayat Aceh</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hikayat Aceh is a Malay court chronicle of the Aceh Sultanate, written in the 17th century in praise of Sultan Iskandar Muda.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-banjar"><span class="enc-name">Hikayat Banjar</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hikayat Banjar is the Malay-language royal chronicle of the Banjar Sultanate of South Kalimantan, surviving in versions from the 17th century onwards.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-merong-mahawangsa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hikayat-merong-mahawangsa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hikayat Merong Mahawangsa</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hikayat Merong Mahawangsa, also called the Kedah Annals, is a Malay chronicle of the kingdom of Kedah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-patani"><span class="enc-name">Hikayat Patani</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hikayat Patani is a Malay chronicle of the sultanate of Pattani on the east coast of the Malay Peninsula, probably compiled in the 17th and 18th centuries.</span></a></li></ul>
